{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Between the evenings he shall burn it.</b> It is known that the fragrance of the incense is more diffusive at the end of the day than the morning incense, and concerning this the verse says (Psalms 141:2): ״תכון תפלתי קטורת לפניך״ — 'Let my prayer be set forth as incense before You,' etc. He did not say 'sin-offering' or 'guilt-offering,' which come for the sin of deed, nor 'burnt-offering,' which comes for the sin of thought, but rather 'incense,' which does not come for sin at all but only for joy — for David sought from HaShem that his prayer be accepted before Him like the incense. Therefore he said (Psalms 141:3): ״שיתה ה׳ שמרה לפי״ — 'Set, O HaShem, a guard for my mouth.' The word שיתה, it seems to me, is connected to the word לפי, as if to say: שיתה ה׳ לפי שמרה לפי — 'Set, O HaShem, to my mouth a guard for my mouth,' meaning that the Holy One, blessed be He, should place in his mouth fitting speech, in the manner of (Exodus 4:11): ״מי שם פה לאדם״ — 'Who set a mouth for man'; and he asks Him also to guard his mouth from unfitting speech. And he said (Psalms 141:3): ״נצרה על דל שפתי״ — 'Keep the door of my lips,' like על דלת שפתי, in the language of (Micah 7:5): ״שמור פתחי פיך״ — 'Guard the openings of your mouth,' for the lips — they open and close like a door, and likewise (Job 41:6): ״דלתי פניו מי פתח״ — 'Who has opened the doors of his face?' — these are the lips of Leviathan. And the word נצרה is an imperative, like (Genesis 25:31): ״מכרה כיום״ — 'Sell it as of today'; (Genesis 25:43) ״שלחה הנער אתי״ — 'Send the youth with me'; and the dagesh in the צד\"י is added as in the dagesh of (Exodus 15:17): ״מקדש ה׳ כוננו ידיך״ — 'The sanctuary of HaShem, Your hands established'; and it is like נצור, and likewise (Proverbs 4:13): ״נצרה כי היא חייך״ — 'Keep her, for she is your life' — the dagesh in the צד\"י is added. (Psalms 141:2): ״משאת כפי מנחת ערב״ — 'The lifting up of my hands, the evening offering' — he asks of Him, blessed be He, that his prayer be found pleasing more than the morning incense, and he mentioned 'the lifting up of my hands' for the incense, which one bears in one's hands, as it is written (Leviticus 16:12): ״ומלא חפניו קטורת סמים דקה״ — 'And he shall fill his handfuls with fine incense of spices.' Or מנחת ערב is from the root of ערבות (sweetness), as in (Malachi 3:4): ״וערבה לה׳ מנחת יהודה וירושלם״ — 'And the offering of Judah and Jerusalem shall be sweet to HaShem.' And since among all the offerings nothing hastens to find favor more than the incense, which sends up smoke immediately, therefore he mentioned at the beginning of the psalm (Psalms 141:1): ״ה׳ קראתיך חושה לי״ — 'HaShem, I have called You; hasten to me.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:41.431149291Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}